{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Do not turn to the elilim.</b> These are the separate intelligences that are below the ten [sefirot], and Scripture calls them elilim because they possess might (אילות) and power, and the duplication of the word is to diminish their power, for their power is not from themselves but from another — namely the distinctive Name, HaShem. And of this the verse says (Psalms 96:5): ״כי כל אלהי העמים אלילים וה׳ שמים עשה״ — for through the creation of the heavens His perfection and power are made known, which come to Him from Himself and not from another.<br><b>And elohi of cast metal.</b> These are the forces of the stars and the constellations, which are below the intelligences, and the idols receive the power of the star or the constellation; therefore He called them elohi of cast metal, in the language of the verse (Isaiah 40:19): ״הפסל נסך חרש״.<br><b>I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> The One who rules over the rank of all existences, all of them; as it is written (Ecclesiastes 5:7): ״כי גבוה מעל גבוה שומר וגבוהים עליהם״.<br>Furthermore, <b>do not turn</b> may include what David said (Psalms 31:16): ״בידך עתותי״ — this comes to allude that the great Name, concerning which they expounded (Exodus 3:15): ״זה שמי לעולם לעלם כתיב״ — this written form embraces in its combination the hours, which are 12 by day and 12 by night, and also embraces the moments (רגעים), concerning which the sign is (Isaiah 27:4): ״אפשעה בה״ — for in the rotation of the first letter of the four [letters of the Name] it includes the numerical value of the moments [רגעים = 288]; therefore the verse concludes ״אני ה׳״ — He warns us not to turn one's heart in all one's hours and moments to anything but the great Name that includes them all. And understand this." "<b>You shall not make for yourselves; I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> He concluded here with three occurrences of ״אני ה׳ אלהיכם,״ and likewise in the commandment concerning the convert, and in the commandment concerning scales, and several other commandments. And the reason for this is that all the commandments are divine, and the practical commandments are all testimonies to the divinity. Therefore our Rabbis, may their memory be blessed, said: whoever engages in Torah alone is like one who has no Elohim — for one who engages in Torah constantly but does not exert himself in the practical commandments has no perfection at all, and is close to heresy, since he does not testify through his deeds to the divinity. And true doctrines are not sustained except through actions, and for this reason the Torah always mentions, in the conclusion of certain commandments, ״אני ה׳ אלהיכם״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The phrase למטה מעשר is read literally as 'below the ten'; רגעים = 288 is left unstated in the Hebrew but implicit in the gematria argument; the allusion to the first letter (יו\"ד = 10) rotated through the four positions of the Name yielding values that sum to 288 is not expanded beyond what he wrote."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:26:49.182935862Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}